# render_widen.cbtable
#   THE bf16 -> fp32 HALF OF THE ACTIVATION STRIP BRIDGE. The SAME THREE KERNEL SOURCES as
#   `eltwise` (resident_eltwise_reader.c / resident_eltwise_compute.c /
#   resident_vector_writer.c), placed with a bf16 INPUT and an fp32 OUTPUT:
#
#     cb  0  Float16_b  2048 B   input A -- a bf16 activation strip, row 0
#     cb  1  Float16_b  2048 B   input B -- a strip of ZEROS, so kVecFnAdd is a copy
#     cb  2  Float16_b  2048 B   scratch, only kVecFnSiluMul uses it; sized to match A
#     cb 16  Float32    4096 B   output -- the same values as fp32 tiles
#
#   READER_CTARGS carries 2048 and WRITER_CTARGS carries 4096. Those are two INDEPENDENT
#   compile-time args on two different processors (build_tensix_program.sh dispatches three
#   separate CTARGS lists), and this is the first program in the tree where they DISAGREE with
#   each other on purpose. Each still agrees with its own circular buffer, which is the actual
#   contract; nothing on the card checks either.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY A PROGRAM AND NOT A MIXED gdn_float32.cbtable
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The obvious move -- make the GDN op's CB_IN Float16_b and read `ssm_qkv` in place -- is the
# one thing this hardware will not do. `emit_tensix_cb_formats.py`'s
# `conditional_destination_format()` is a WHOLE-PROGRAM decision: Float32 CBs unpack into Tf32
# source registers only when `dst_accum_mode` is on AND **every** CB of the program is
# Float32. One bf16 CB anywhere in the table demotes the recurrent state and the conv weights
# to Float16_b in SrcA, which is the one thing the GDN family exists to avoid -- it carries
# state across tokens and the CPU oracle accumulates in fp64 for that reason. So the format
# change has to happen in a DIFFERENT program, and this is it.
#
# In THIS program the same demotion is free: the input really is bf16, so unpacking it into a
# bf16 source register loses nothing, and the pack from an fp32 DST to a Float32 CB is exact.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE FUNCTION IS kVecFnAdd AGAINST A ZERO STRIP, AND THAT IS WHY THIS NEEDS NO KERNEL CHANGE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# There is no identity in `ft_tt_op_vector_function` and adding one would change
# `resident_eltwise_compute.c`, which would make every eltwise-family program stale by content
# provenance and cost a re-validation of the shipped `eltwise` binary. `x + 0` is exact in
# every format on this list, and a zero operand costs nothing to produce: every strip
# allocator in Pantheon already stages its allocation zeroed, so the B buffer is allocated and
# never written. The cost is one extra DRAM read stream, which this op is nowhere near bound
# by.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# dst_accum_mode 1 -- required, for the OUTPUT side
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `derive_format_roles()` gives a Float32 CB `pack_src = Float32` only when fp32 destination
# accumulation is on; with it off the packer's source would be the conditional format
# (Float16_b) and the fp32 output would be a bf16 value stored in four bytes -- a render that
# renders nothing, with nothing refusing. The all-bf16 `eltwise` table ships `dst_accum_mode 1`
# already, so this is the same setting and not a new risk.
#
# math_fidelity 4: HiFi4, same as every other table here. An add against zero is exact at any
# level; the setting is kept uniform so a fidelity difference is never a candidate when this
# program is compared against `eltwise`.
#
#   Format: tools/emit_tensix_cb_formats.py
#   cb <index> <format> <tile_r> <tile_c> <face_r> <num_faces>

math_fidelity  4
approx         0
dst_accum_mode 1

cb  0  Float16_b 32 32 16 4
cb  1  Float16_b 32 32 16 4
cb  2  Float16_b 32 32 16 4
cb 16  Float32   32 32 16 4
